# The life a started thread owes its wait, held as a handle of C, as Rebel's
# `threads` holds it (`packages/rebel/0.1.0/files/threads/internal/lives.hero`):
# a block of one byte from `malloc`, which `acquires free`, given back by
# `free`, which `consumes` it, so the language's count of C handles tells a
# thread never waited for. The byte is never read or written.
extern "stdlib.h"
    record Life tag void
    function malloc(size: u64) -> Life acquires free
    function free(p: Life consumes)

# A new life, which the program then owes `end`.
function begin() -> Life
    return malloc(1)

# Ends `life`, which may not be read again.
function end(@life: Life)
    free(life)
